<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Eleonora Fonseca Pimentel</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1752–1799</span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Eleonora Fonseca Pimentel was a Neapolitan poet and journalist, born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> to a Portuguese noble family, who became a leading figure of the Parthenopean Republic of 1799. Once a member of the royal court, she turned to republicanism and edited the republic's official newspaper, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/monitore-napoletano">Monitore Napoletano</a>. After the royalist reconquest she was hanged in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a> in August 1799.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/eleonora-fonseca-pimentel.jpg" alt="Eleonora Fonseca Pimentel" width="320" height="393" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Immaculate 21:53, 5 March 2008 (UTC) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Eleonora_Fonseca_Pimentel.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
